# The `define` is load-bearing, not scenery: the first version of the guard
# that refuses this module did `if u[0] == "func": return 0` inside its scan
# loop — a return where a skip was meant — so it stopped at the first define
# and never saw the conditional binding below. A module that declares a
# function before a conditional binding is the NORMAL shape (stdlib pkg:
# define line 54, binding line 573), so without this define the fixture passes
# against a guard that is blind to every real module.
define f() as:
    return 1

a is 1
if a == 1:
    b is 2
c is 3
